\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The two-board Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) testbed, showing the Controller and Simulator ESP32 boards connected by two analogue signal paths and a dedicated reset line.}}{31}{figure.caption.16}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Bang-bang controller temperature response (native variant). The controller oscillates around the 40\textdegree C target with an amplitude of $\approx $4.2\textdegree C and a period of $\approx $2.9~s.}}{46}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces PID controller temperature response (native variant). After an initial overshoot to $\approx $42\textdegree C, the controller settles to a mean of 40.0\textdegree C with $\sigma \approx 0.3$\textdegree C.}}{46}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Step-response temperature profile (native variant). Six heating cycles are visible, each peaking at $\approx $60\textdegree C before cooling back to $\approx $36\textdegree C.}}{47}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Discrete-time timeline for the end-to-end latency measurement. The controller issues a heater command, the simulator samples it on a 50\,ms tick, and the controller detects the resulting temperature rise on its 10\,ms reader cycle. The observed mean latency is 111.7\,ms for native execution and about 121\,ms for both WAMR modes.}}{48}{figure.caption.26}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Shared memory: host reads and writes a single container's linear memory through a native pointer.}}{68}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Queue: producer and consumer tasks exchange fixed-size messages through a FreeRTOS queue.}}{69}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Stream buffer: producer appends bytes to a ring that the consumer drains at its own pace.}}{69}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Host-mediated read: producer and consumer exchange messages through a native buffer owned by the host.}}{70}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Host-driven invocation: host drives the loop and invokes exported container functions with each message.}}{71}{figure.caption.49}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of native execution, WAMR interpreter, and WAMR AOT on the ESP32. AOT recovers most of the interpreter's execution overhead, but not its SRAM cost.}}{78}{figure.caption.59}%
